/**
 * \defgroup distrib_gaussian1 Gaussian distribution (mean-sd)
 * \ingroup distrib
 *
 * Defined in `<laplace/distrib/gaussian1.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib "gaussian1",
 * the univariate Gaussian distribution provides log-density, parameter and observation derivatives up
 * to order \f$6\f$, expected Fisher information with parameter derivatives up to order \f$4\f$, and
 * cumulative, log-cumulative, and quantile functions with derivatives up to order \f$6\f$. Every
 * method is marked `LAPLACE_HD` so evaluators run identically on host CPUs and Kokkos device
 * backends; see \ref distrib_core for the shared interface, `sym_tensor` storage, and Kokkos
 * execution patterns. Against 400-bit `mpmath` references, `log_cdf()` and `log_ccdf()` reach at
 * most \f$30.9\f$ and \f$29.7\f$ scaled ulp over the full suite and \f$4.81\f$ and \f$4.38\f$ in
 * the tails.
 *
 * ## Classic form and regularity
 *
 * The classic Gaussian density in mean \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$ and standard deviation
 * \f$\sigma > 0\f$ is
 * \f[
 * f(y; \mu, \sigma) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}\,\sigma} \exp\!\left(-\frac{(y - \mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}\right), \qquad y \in (-\infty, \infty).
 * \f]
 * The library uses this mean-sd parametrization directly, with `center()` returning \f$\mu\f$ and
 * `scale()` returning \f$\sigma\f$. The distribution is smooth everywhere: `y_regularity` is
 * `distrib_regularity::infinitely_smooth`, `moves_support` is `false`, both parameter descriptors
 * mark `regularity` and `cdf_regularity` as `infinitely_smooth`, and `param_kinks()` and
 * `y_kinks()` return zero kinks.
 *
 * ## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions
 *
 * Let \f$z = (y - \mu)/\sigma\f$. The log-density is
 * \f[
 * \ell(\mu, \sigma; y) = -\log\sigma - \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi) - \frac{z^2}{2}.
 * \f]
 * For parameter derivatives of order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$, the mixed partial with \f$a = K - b\f$
 * derivatives in \f$\mu\f$ and \f$b\f$ in \f$\sigma\f$ vanishes for \f$a > 2\f$, and otherwise equals
 * \f$(-1)^b (b + 1)! / \sigma^{b + 2}\f$ for \f$a = 2\f$,
 * \f$(-1)^b (b + 1)! \, z / \sigma^{b + 1}\f$ for \f$a = 1\f$, and
 * \f$(-1)^{K - 1} (K - 1)! / \sigma^K + (-1)^K (K + 1)! \, z^2 / (2\sigma^K)\f$ for \f$a = 0\f$.
 * Mixed partials `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) exploit translation invariance
 * \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$, multiplying the \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ tensor by
 * \f$(-1)^J\f$ with \f$a = K - b + J\f$.
 *
 * The expected Fisher information is diagonal, \f$I(\mu, \sigma) = \operatorname{diag}(1/\sigma^2, 2/\sigma^2)\f$,
 * stored in `sym_tensor<2, 2>` with `v[0] = 1 / (sigma * sigma)`, `v[1] = 0`, and `v[2] = 2 / (sigma * sigma)`.
 * Only pure \f$\sigma\f$-derivatives survive, giving
 * \f$\partial^K I / \partial\sigma^K = (-1)^K (K + 1)! / \sigma^{K + 2} \operatorname{diag}(1, 2)\f$
 * for \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$.
 *
 * Cumulative functions wrap the standard normal primitives in `laplace::math`:
 * `cdf(y) = normal_cdf(z)`, `ccdf(y) = normal_ccdf(z)`, and `quantile(p) = mu + sigma * normal_quantile(p)`.
 * For `log_cdf(y)` and `log_ccdf(y)`, central points (\f$|z| < 4\f$) call `log_normal_cdf(z)` and
 * `log_normal_ccdf(z)`. In the deep tails (\f$z \le -4\f$ for `log_cdf`, \f$z \ge 4\f$ for `log_ccdf`),
 * setting \f$x = -\operatorname{sgn}(z)\,z \ge 4\f$, \f$t = -x\f$, and \f$w = 1/x^2 = \sigma^2/(y-\mu)^2\f$,
 * the identity
 * \f[
 * \log\Phi(t) = -\frac{t^2}{2} - \log x - \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi) - w + S_2(t), \qquad S_2(t) = \log(x M_0(x)) + w,
 * \f]
 * with Mills ratio \f$M_0(x) = \int_0^\infty \exp(-x s - s^2/2)\,\mathrm{d}s\f$, separates the exact
 * closed-form leading terms from \f$S_2(t) = O(x^{-4})\f$ and avoids subtracting \f$\log\Phi(t)\f$
 * against \f$-t^2/2\f$ when differentiating up to order \f$6\f$ via `log_normal_cdf_d1()` ..
 * `log_normal_cdf_d6()`.
 *
 * ## Accuracy
 *
 * Measured on the CPU `gcc` build against 400-bit `mpmath` references (details in `docs/numerics.md`),
 * scaled ulp errors (see \ref accuracy and \ref distrib_core) stay within:
 *
 * | Method | Suite | Max scaled ulp | Test bound |
 * | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `log_cdf` | `distrib_log_cdf.csv` | \f$30.9\f$ | \f$46.4\f$ |
 * | `log_ccdf` | `distrib_log_cdf.csv` | \f$29.7\f$ | \f$44.6\f$ |
 * | `log_cdf` | `distrib_log_cdf_tails.csv` (\f$z \le -1.5\f$) | \f$4.81\f$ | \f$7.3\f$ |
 * | `log_ccdf` | `distrib_log_cdf_tails.csv` (\f$z \ge 1.5\f$) | \f$4.38\f$ | \f$6.6\f$ |
 *
 * Across parameter and observation tests, 5-point finite differences confirm `logpdf_dk`,
 * `expected_info_dk`, and `logpdf_dy_dk` within relative tolerance \f$10^{-8}\f$, and numerical score
 * variance matches `expected_info()` within \f$10^{-12}\f$.
 *
 * ## References
 *
 * - F. Johansson and others, *mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic*, http://mpmath.org/.
 */

/**
 * \defgroup distrib_laplace1 Laplace distribution (location-scale)
 * \ingroup distrib
 *
 * Defined in `<laplace/distrib/laplace1.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::laplace1_distrib "laplace1",
 * the univariate Laplace distribution provides log-density and mixed derivatives up to order \f$6\f$,
 * expected Fisher information with derivatives up to order \f$4\f$, and cumulative, log-cumulative,
 * and quantile functions with derivatives up to order \f$6\f$. All evaluators are marked `LAPLACE_HD`
 * for host and Kokkos device execution; see \ref distrib_core for common interface and storage
 * conventions. Against 400-bit `mpmath` references, `log_cdf()` and `log_ccdf()` reach at most
 * \f$11.2\f$ and \f$9.86\f$ scaled ulp across the full suite.
 *
 * ## Classic form and regularity
 *
 * The classic Laplace density in location \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$ and scale \f$b > 0\f$ is
 * \f[
 * f(y; \mu, b) = \frac{1}{2b} \exp\!\left(-\frac{|y - \mu|}{b}\right), \qquad y \in (-\infty, \infty).
 * \f]
 * The library identifies \f$\sigma = b\f$, with `center()` returning \f$\mu\f$ and `scale()` returning
 * \f$\sigma\f$. The cusp at \f$y = \mu\f$ causes a jump of \f$2/\sigma\f$ in the first derivative of
 * \f$\log f\f$: `y_regularity` is \f$0\f$ (continuous, piecewise smooth), \f$\mu\f$ has `regularity = 0`
 * and `cdf_regularity = 1` (since \f$F\f$ is \f$C^1\f$), \f$\sigma\f$ is `infinitely_smooth`, and
 * `moves_support` is `false`. Both `param_kinks(0, y)` and `y_kinks()` report one knot of
 * `continuous_order = 0` at \f$\mu = y\f$ (or \f$y = \mu\f$), while `param_kinks(1, y)` has count \f$0\f$.
 *
 * ## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions
 *
 * With \f$z = (y - \mu)/\sigma\f$, the log-density is \f$\ell(\mu, \sigma; y) = -\log(2\sigma) - |z|\f$.
 * Parameter and mixed derivatives use the symmetric convention \f$\operatorname{sgn}(0) = 0\f$ at
 * \f$y = \mu\f$. Away from the kink, for \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$, the partial with \f$a = K - b\f$
 * derivatives in \f$\mu\f$ and \f$b\f$ in \f$\sigma\f$ vanishes when \f$a \ge 2\f$, equals
 * \f$(-1)^b b! \, \operatorname{sgn}(z) / \sigma^{b + 1}\f$ when \f$a = 1\f$, and equals
 * \f$(-1)^K (K - 1)! / \sigma^K + (-1)^{K + 1} K! \, |z| / \sigma^K\f$ when \f$a = 0\f$. Mixed
 * partials `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) apply \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.
 *
 * Although \f$\ell\f$ is non-differentiable at \f$y = \mu\f$, the score variance defines the regular
 * Fisher information \f$I(\mu, \sigma) = \operatorname{diag}(1/\sigma^2, 1/\sigma^2)\f$, stored in
 * `sym_tensor<2, 2>` with `v[0] = v[2] = 1 / (sigma * sigma)` and `v[1] = 0`. For \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$,
 * only pure \f$\sigma\f$-derivatives are nonzero, equal to \f$(-1)^K (K + 1)! / \sigma^{K + 2} I_2\f$.
 *
 * The cumulative and quantile functions split around \f$z = 0\f$:
 * \f[
 * F(y) = \begin{cases} \frac{1}{2} e^{z}, & z \le 0, \\ 1 - \frac{1}{2} e^{-z}, & z > 0, \end{cases}
 * \qquad
 * F^{-1}(p) = \begin{cases} \mu + \sigma \log(2p), & p \le \frac{1}{2}, \\ \mu - \sigma \log(2(1 - p)), & p > \frac{1}{2}. \end{cases}
 * \f]
 * On the linear side (\f$z < 0\f$ for `log_cdf`, \f$z > 0\f$ for `log_ccdf`), \f$\log F = -\log 2 + z\f$
 * and \f$\log(1 - F) = -\log 2 - z\f$ have exact closed-form derivatives. On the opposite side, two
 * regimes avoid cancellation:
 * - For \f$0 \le |z| < 0.25\f$, writing \f$q = 1/(2e^{|z|} - 1)\f$, polynomial derivatives
 *   \f$\mathrm{d}^j \log(1 - \frac{1}{2}e^{-|z|}) / \mathrm{d}|z|^j = (-1)^{j - 1} P_j(q)\f$ with
 *   \f$P_1(q) = q\f$ and \f$P_{j+1}(q) = -q(1 + q) P_j'(q)\f$ combine with \f$\partial^k (-|z|) / \partial\theta^k\f$
 *   via Faà di Bruno composition.
 * - For \f$|z| \ge 0.25\f$, the Mercator series \f$-\sum_{n = 1}^{\infty} (2^{-n}/n) \exp(c_n/\sigma)\f$
 *   with \f$c_n = n s (y - \mu)\f$ (\f$s = -1\f$ for `log_cdf`, \f$s = 1\f$ for `log_ccdf`) has
 *   mixed partials evaluated cleanly through associated Laguerre polynomials \f$L_b^{(a - 1)}(n |z|)\f$
 *   computed by the recurrence \f$(k + 1) L_{k + 1}^{(\alpha)}(u) = (2k + 1 + \alpha - u) L_k^{(\alpha)}(u) - (k + \alpha) L_{k - 1}^{(\alpha)}(u)\f$,
 *   verified symbolically with SymPy 1.14.0.
 *
 * ## Accuracy
 *
 * Measured on the CPU `gcc` build against 400-bit `mpmath` references (details in `docs/numerics.md`):
 *
 * | Method | Suite | Max scaled ulp | Test bound |
 * | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `log_cdf` | `distrib_log_cdf.csv` | \f$11.2\f$ | \f$16.8\f$ |
 * | `log_ccdf` | `distrib_log_cdf.csv` | \f$9.86\f$ | \f$14.8\f$ |
 * | `log_cdf` | `distrib_log_cdf_tails.csv` (\f$z \ge 0.05\f$) | \f$16.7\f$ | \f$25.0\f$ |
 * | `log_ccdf` | `distrib_log_cdf_tails.csv` (\f$z \le -0.05\f$) | \f$6.05\f$ | \f$9.1\f$ |
 *
 * Away from \f$y = \mu\f$, 5-point finite differences verify `logpdf_dk`, `expected_info_dk`, and
 * `logpdf_dy_dk` within relative tolerance \f$10^{-8}\f$, and score-variance quadrature split at
 * \f$y = \mu\f$ matches `expected_info()` within \f$10^{-12}\f$.
 *
 * ## References
 *
 * - F. Johansson and others, *mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic*, http://mpmath.org/.
 * - A. Meurer and others, *SymPy: symbolic computing in Python*, PeerJ Computer Science 3 (2017), e103.
 */

/**
 * \defgroup distrib_poisson Poisson distribution (mean)
 * \ingroup distrib
 *
 * Defined in `<laplace/distrib/poisson.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::poisson_distrib "poisson",
 * the univariate Poisson distribution provides log-mass and parameter derivatives up to order \f$6\f$,
 * expected Fisher information with derivatives up to order \f$4\f$, and cumulative and log-cumulative
 * functions with derivatives up to order \f$6\f$. All methods carry `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos
 * device execution; see \ref distrib_core for shared conventions. Against 300-bit `mpmath` references,
 * `log_cdf()` and `log_ccdf()` reach at most \f$3.84 \times 10^3\f$ and \f$867\f$ scaled ulp across
 * the full suite.
 *
 * ## Classic form and regularity
 *
 * The classic Poisson probability mass function with rate \f$\lambda > 0\f$ is
 * \f[
 * p(y; \lambda) = \frac{\lambda^y e^{-\lambda}}{y!}, \qquad y \in \{0, 1, 2, \dots\}.
 * \f]
 * The library names this mean parameter \f$\mu = \lambda\f$, with `center()` returning \f$\mu\f$ and
 * `scale()` returning \f$\sqrt{\mu}\f$. Because support is discrete on \f$\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}\f$,
 * `poisson` exposes no observation regularity or `y_kinks()` method (`logpdf_dy_dk` is omitted as well),
 * while `moves_support` is `false`, \f$\mu\f$ has `regularity = infinitely_smooth` and
 * `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `param_kinks()` always returns count \f$0\f$.
 *
 * ## Log-mass, Fisher information, and cumulative functions
 *
 * At \f$y = 0\f$, `logpmf(0)` returns \f$-\mu\f$. For integer \f$y \ge 1\f$, `logpmf(y)` uses the
 * saddle-point deviance identity instead of subtracting \f$\log\Gamma(y + 1)\f$:
 * \f[
 * \ell(\mu; y) = -\mu D(y/\mu) - \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi y) - \delta(y), \qquad D(z) = z \log z - z + 1,
 * \f]
 * where \f$\delta(y)\f$ is the Stirling error. Writing \f$w = (y - \mu)/\mu\f$, when \f$|w| < 0.25\f$
 * the deviance is evaluated as \f$y - \mu + \mu w \sum_{n = 2}^{\infty} (-1)^n w^n / (n(n - 1))\f$
 * in \f$\mu\f$-space and summed as \f$\mu w^2 \sum \dots\f$, avoiding catastrophic cancellation when
 * \f$y \approx \mu\f$ is large. Parameter derivatives of order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ are exact:
 * \f$\partial\ell/\partial\mu = y/\mu - 1\f$ and
 * \f$\partial^K\ell/\partial\mu^K = (-1)^{K - 1} (K - 1)! \, y / \mu^K\f$ for \f$K \ge 2\f$.
 *
 * The scalar expected Fisher information is \f$I(\mu) = 1/\mu\f$, stored in `sym_tensor<1, 2>` with
 * `v[0] = 1 / mu`, and its \f$K\f$-th derivative (\f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$) is
 * \f$(-1)^K K! / \mu^{K + 1}\f$.
 *
 * For \f$y < 0\f$, `cdf()` and `log_ccdf()` return \f$0\f$, `ccdf()` returns \f$1\f$, `log_cdf()` returns
 * \f$-\infty\f$, `log_cdf_dk()` fills quiet NaN, and `log_ccdf_dk()` returns \f$0\f$. For \f$y \ge 0\f$,
 * letting \f$k = \lfloor y \rfloor\f$, the cumulative and complementary probabilities are the
 * regularized incomplete gamma functions \f$F(y) = Q(k + 1, \mu)\f$ and \f$1 - F(y) = P(k + 1, \mu)\f$.
 * Their log-values and derivatives of order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ call the pure \f$x\f$-entries of
 * \ref laplace::math::own::log_gamma_q_mixed_d1 "log_gamma_q_mixed_d1()" .. `log_gamma_q_mixed_d6()` and
 * \ref laplace::math::own::log_gamma_p_mixed_d1 "log_gamma_p_mixed_d1()" .. `log_gamma_p_mixed_d6()`
 * at \f$(s, x) = (k + 1, \mu)\f$.
 *
 * ## Accuracy
 *
 * Measured on the CPU `gcc` build against 300-bit `mpmath` references (details in `docs/numerics.md`):
 *
 * | Method | Suite | Max scaled ulp | Test bound |
 * | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `log_cdf` | `distrib_log_cdf.csv` | \f$3.84 \times 10^3\f$ | \f$5.8 \times 10^3\f$ |
 * | `log_ccdf` | `distrib_log_cdf.csv` | \f$867\f$ | \f$1.3 \times 10^3\f$ |
 *
 * Finite differences confirm `logpdf_dk` and `expected_info_dk` within relative tolerance \f$10^{-8}\f$,
 * and direct summation of \f$p(y;\mu) (\partial\ell/\partial\mu)^2\f$ agrees with `expected_info()`
 * within \f$10^{-13}\f$.
 *
 * ## References
 *
 * - F. Johansson and others, *mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic*, http://mpmath.org/.
 */

/**
 * \defgroup distrib_gamma1 Gamma distribution (mean-dispersion)
 * \ingroup distrib
 *
 * Defined in `<laplace/distrib/gamma1.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::gamma1_distrib "gamma1",
 * the univariate gamma distribution provides log-density and mixed derivatives up to order \f$6\f$,
 * expected Fisher information with derivatives up to order \f$4\f$, and cumulative, log-cumulative,
 * and quantile functions with derivatives up to order \f$6\f$. Every method carries `LAPLACE_HD` for
 * host and Kokkos device execution; see \ref distrib_core for common interface and `sym_tensor`
 * storage conventions. Against 300-bit `mpmath` references, `log_cdf()` and `log_ccdf()` reach at
 * most \f$379\f$ and \f$356\f$ scaled ulp across the suite.
 *
 * ## Classic form and regularity
 *
 * In shape \f$s > 0\f$ and scale \f$\theta > 0\f$, the classic gamma density on \f$y \in (0, \infty)\f$ is
 * \f[
 * f(y; s, \theta) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(s)\,\theta^s} y^{s - 1} e^{-y/\theta}.
 * \f]
 * Substituting mean \f$\mu = s\theta > 0\f$ and dispersion \f$\phi = 1/s > 0\f$ (so \f$s = 1/\phi\f$,
 * \f$\theta = \mu\phi\f$, and \f$\operatorname{Var}(Y) = \phi\mu^2\f$) gives
 * \f[
 * f(y; \mu, \phi) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1/\phi)\,(\mu\phi)^{1/\phi}} y^{1/\phi - 1} \exp\!\left(-\frac{y}{\mu\phi}\right).
 * \f]
 * Here `center()` returns \f$\mu\f$ and `scale()` returns \f$\mu\sqrt{\phi}\f$. The distribution is
 * smooth on \f$(0, \infty)\f$: `y_regularity` is `infinitely_smooth`, `moves_support` is `false`, both
 * parameters mark `regularity` and `cdf_regularity` as `infinitely_smooth`, and `param_kinks()` and
 * `y_kinks()` return zero kinks.
 *
 * ## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions
 *
 * For \f$y \le 0\f$, `logpdf(y)` and `log_cdf(y)` return \f$-\infty\f$, `cdf(y)` and `log_ccdf(y)` return
 * \f$0\f$, and `ccdf(y)` returns \f$1\f$. For \f$y > 0\f$, with \f$s = 1/\phi\f$, the log-density splits into
 * separate parameter contributions:
 * \f[
 * \ell(\mu, \phi; y) = s G(\mu) + H(\phi) - \log y - \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi), \qquad
 * G(\mu) = -D\!\left(\frac{y}{\mu}\right), \qquad H(\phi) = \frac{1}{2}\log s - \delta(s),
 * \f]
 * where \f$\delta(s)\f$ is the Stirling error and \f$D(z) = z - 1 - \log z\f$. Setting \f$w = (y - \mu)/\mu\f$,
 * \f$D(y/\mu)\f$ uses the series \f$\sum_{n = 2}^{\infty} (-1)^n w^n / n\f$ for \f$|w| < 0.25\f$,
 * \f$w - \log(y/\mu)\f$ for \f$w < -0.5\f$, and \f$w - \log(1 + w)\f$ otherwise, preventing cancellation
 * when \f$\phi\f$ is small and \f$y \approx \mu\f$. Parameter derivatives combine closed-form
 * \f$\partial^a G/\partial\mu^a\f$, \f$s^{(b)} = (-1)^b b! / \phi^{b + 1}\f$, and Faà di Bruno composition
 * of \f$\delta^{(m)}(s)\f$ with \f$s^{(b)}\f$. Mixed partials `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$)
 * differentiate only \f$-s y/\mu + (s - 1)\log y\f$.
 *
 * The expected Fisher information is diagonal,
 * \f[
 * I(\mu, \phi) = \operatorname{diag}\!\left(\frac{s}{\mu^2},\; \frac{s^2}{2} + s^4 \delta''(s)\right),
 * \f]
 * stored in `sym_tensor<2, 2>` with `v[1] = 0` and `v[2] = s * s * (0.5 + s * s * stirling_error_d2(s))`,
 * canceling the leading \f$s^2\f$ cancellation in \f$\psi'(s) - 1/s\f$. Its derivatives of order
 * \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$ propagate \f$s^{(b)}\f$ on \f$I_{\mu\mu}\f$ and apply Faà di Bruno plus
 * Leibniz differentiation on \f$I_{\phi\phi}\f$.
 *
 * For \f$y > 0\f$, `cdf(y) = gamma_p(s, s y / \mu)`, `ccdf(y) = gamma_q(s, s y / \mu)`, and
 * `quantile(p) = mu * phi * gamma_p_inv(s, p)`. Because \f$x = s y / \mu\f$ scales with \f$s = 1/\phi\f$,
 * differentiating \f$\log P(s, s r)\f$ or \f$\log Q(s, s r)\f$ directly in \f$(s, x)\f$ loses \f$O(\phi^{-2})\f$
 * accuracy to chain-rule cancellation. Instead, `log_cdf_dk()` and `log_ccdf_dk()` evaluate fixed-ratio
 * partials \f$R_{ij} = \partial^{i + j} \log P(s, s r) / \partial s^i \partial r^j\f$ via
 * \ref laplace::math::own::log_gamma_p_ratio_mixed_d1 "log_gamma_p_ratio_mixed_d1()" .. `_d6()` and
 * \ref laplace::math::own::log_gamma_q_ratio_mixed_d1 "log_gamma_q_ratio_mixed_d1()" .. `_d6()`, then
 * compose with \f$s = 1/\phi\f$ and \f$r = y/\mu\f$ through `bell_partial` in \ref faa_di_bruno
 * (generated with SymPy 1.14.0).
 *
 * ## Accuracy
 *
 * Measured on the CPU `gcc` build against 300-bit `mpmath` references across \f$\phi \in \{0.02, 0.3, 4\}\f$
 * (details in `docs/numerics.md`):
 *
 * | Method | Suite | Max scaled ulp | Test bound |
 * | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `log_cdf` | `distrib_log_cdf.csv` | \f$379\f$ | \f$570\f$ |
 * | `log_ccdf` | `distrib_log_cdf.csv` | \f$356\f$ | \f$540\f$ |
 *
 * Finite differences agree within relative tolerance \f$10^{-8}\f$ for `logpdf_dk` and
 * `expected_info_dk` and \f$10^{-7}\f$ for `logpdf_dy_dk`; tanh-sinh and exp-sinh score-variance
 * quadrature split at \f$y = \mu\f$ agrees with `expected_info()` within \f$10^{-12}\f$ for \f$I_{\mu\mu}\f$
 * and \f$10^{-10}\f$ for \f$I_{\phi\phi}\f$.
 *
 * ## References
 *
 * - F. Johansson and others, *mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic*, http://mpmath.org/.
 * - A. Meurer and others, *SymPy: symbolic computing in Python*, PeerJ Computer Science 3 (2017), e103.
 */
